From 75462ca24acf55e8c4d0c76ceae55e44c42dcdcc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:48:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 15/24] l2r-local: entry 23-g: tests: deep worklist drops, and
 the release order

Sixth step: tests of what the switch to worklist drops (23-a..23-f)
promises.

- frontend/worklist_drop_depth.rr (+ .c): on a 256 KiB stack, frees
  10^6-level one-sided trees of two recursive fields (left- and
  right-deep), a chain of a 64-arm enum whose recursive member comes
  first (the shape of a closure enum capturing a closure, then a value),
  and compositions of two recursive fields; peak RSS must grow by less
  than 16 MiB during each drop. On l2r-base7 (retcon drops) the
  left-deep tree alone grew it by 84 MiB; with worklist drops none of the
  four grows it by more than a few MiB of allocator noise. At -O
  aggressive and -O none.
- sanitizer/e2e/worklist_drop_depth.rr: the same frees at 10^5 levels
  under ASan (every cell freed once, nothing leaked), both nullary
  encodings.
- runtime/drop_member_order.mlir now runs with worklist-drops, and its
  driver expects the worklist order: a cell's members in field order at
  its visit, the next cell's box after them, so the chains' handles come
  from the root down (the retcon order released a cell's prefix before
  and its suffix after the whole rest of the chain). New cases: a
  complete binary tree of 7 cells with one handle each (dead children are
  released last first: root, right subtree, left subtree); a cell under a
  dead parent whose left child, right child or both are shared with the
  driver (only decremented, released at the end); and a cell with a
  child held inline after its other children (an `OrderInl [value]`
  record), as a root and as a dead child: the inline child, deferred
  last, comes first. The test no longer covers the retcon order, which
  no pipeline uses any more; that is accepted until retcon is deleted.
